      ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral \n\n$$\n\\int 5 xe^{- 2 x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n",
      ":question/worked-solution": "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\int 5 xe^{- 2 x}\\text{d}x$ as $\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore, we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =5 x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=5 \\\\ {v}^{′} & =e^{- 2 x}\\, & ⟹\\,v=- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int 5 x\\cdot e^{- 2 x}\\,\\text{d}x & =5 x\\cdot \\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)-\\int \\left(- \\frac{e^{- 2 x}}{2}\\right)\\cdot 5\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5 xe^{- 2 x}\\,\\text{d}x & =- \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 xe^{- 2 x}+\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\int e^{- 2 x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =- \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 5 xe^{- 2 x}-\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot e^{- 2 x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCollecting the linear terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5 xe^{- 2 x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x & =-\\frac{5 x e^{- 2 x}}{2} - \\frac{5 e^{- 2 x}}{4}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
